{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c5ab","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07dba-037c-7562-bb6d-27534398ea11"},"sequence":304,"recorded_at":1780531400000603,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"0442ca47-9067-45d4-a281-7f472a3b7616","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"0442ca47-9067-45d4-a281-7f472a3b7616","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"0442ca47-9067-45d4-a281-7f472a3b7616"},"call_id":"call_01a07dbaad9b7ea1a7d5c740cfaa28f3","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260830-35dbcs/worktree/internal/specdoc/SPEC.md`.\n(7929 line(s) above)\n7930|inert opaque history when the v3 reader permits it, and reports TerminalBackend\n7931|evidence unsupported rather than empty; it cannot activate or restore from the\n7932|unknown binding. A v4/v2 negotiation performs core sync under v2 and reports\n7933|both directory and TerminalBackend evidence unsupported. No peer coerces a\n7934|namespace, event, or backend identity across majors, and unsupported activation\n7935|never prevents safe browse/sync of contracts that were negotiated.\n7936|\n7937|## 12. Workspace replication\n7938|\n7939|### 12.1 Workspace snapshot\n7940|\n7941|Every checkpoint references a workspace-group Transfer Manifest. The snapshot\n7942|MUST be <code>kind = workspace_group</code> and carry the exact Section 10.4\n7943|<code>WorkspaceSnapshot</code>; every referenced member tree is in its transitive\n7944|child closure. That wire representation contains the exact state needed to\n7945|resume:\n7946|\n7947|- each required repository or managed directory;\n7948|- sanitized remote URLs;\n7949|- HEAD object ID and symbolic branch, or detached state;\n7950|- reachable local commits needed for HEAD and submodules;\n7951|- staged index state;\n7952|- unstaged tracked content;\n7953|- untracked non-ignored content;\n7954|- configured agent project files even when ignored, subject to exclusions;\n7955|- recursive submodule identity, commit, and working state;\n7956|- repository-relative cwd; and\n7957|- the workspace-group sharing/worktree policy.\n7958|\n7959|Ignored build outputs are excluded by default. An ignored file is included only\n7960|when named by a project/provider include rule and classified as non-secret.\n7961|M1 Git closure is complete only when tracked, dirty-index, staged, unstaged,\n7962|untracked, ignored-policy, symlink, and submodule state is represented and\n7963|validated. Omitting any one class is <code>workspace_conflict</code>; a clean\n7964|HEAD or object pack is not a proxy for working-copy closure.\n7965|\n7966|### 12.2 Git workspace schema\n7967|\n7968|The Git wire schema is the exact <code>kind = git</code>\n7969|<code>WorkspaceSnapshotMember</code> and its closed nested types in Section\n7970|10.4. There is no second prose-only Git descriptor. In particular,\n7971|<code>head</code> distinguishes branch, detached, and unborn states;\n7972|<code>object_pack</code> and its inventory carry offline object closure; the raw\n7973|<code>index</code> blob plus logical <code>GitIndexEntry</code> array preserve\n7974|stages and flags; <code>working_tree_manifest_id</code> carries actual tracked,\n7975|unstaged, untracked, and explicitly included ignored bytes; and submodule,\n7976|feature, cwd, and agent-config fields are all required members of that same\n7977|Transfer Manifest object.\n7978|\n7979|Remote URLs MUST be sanitized. User info containing a password/token, credential\n7980|helpers with embedded secrets, and machine-local paths MUST be removed.\n7981|Repository config MUST be reconstructed from an allowlist: remotes, branch\n7982|tracking, object format, safe submodule URLs, and required worktree extensions.\n7983|Hooks, credential configuration, local include files, and arbitrary commands\n7984|MUST NOT be copied.\n7985|\n7986|The <code>WS-GIT-ROUNDTRIP-1</code> and\n7987|<code>WS-TREE-ROUNDTRIP-1</code> fixtures in Section 10.4 are normative inputs\n7988|to <code>AC-WORK-001</code>/<code>AC-WORK-002</code>. An implementation MUST\n7989|parse those exact descriptors before invoking Git or writing a destination;\n7990|it MUST reject their listed negative mutations without relying on a live\n7991|repository to fill missing schema facts.\n7992|\n7993|### 12.3 Git capture and materialization\n7994|\n7995|Capture MUST quiesce agent input and filesystem-mutating provider work. It MUST\n7996|read Git state consistently and fail if HEAD, index, or included file digests\n7997|change during capture.\n7998|\n7999|Materialization MUST:\n8000|\n8001|1. create or validate a managed repository/worktree;\n8002|2. configure sanitized remotes;\n8003|3. import each repository's object pack into that repository's object database\n8004|   only and verify its required OIDs there; a superproject gitlink is validated\n8005|   against the child descriptor/pack, never by co-mingling child objects into\n8006|   the parent database;\n8007|4. establish the exact branch or detached HEAD without contacting a remote;\n8008|5. construct a temporary index from the descriptor and atomically install it;\n8009|6. materialize actual working-tree bytes, modes, and symlinks;\n8010|7. recurse through submodules using their logical mappings; and\n8011|8. compare resulting HEAD, index, status, cwd, and content digests with the\n8012|   descriptor.\n8013|\n8014|An implementation MAY use the Git executable, a Go library, or both, but the\n8015|observable result is fixed by this section. Unsupported Git extensions, case\n8016|collisions, inaccessible filters, or object-format mismatches MUST fail closed.\n8017|Network fetch MAY be offered as an explicit repair, but materialization MUST NOT\n8018|silently depend on it.\n8019|\n8020|Staged and unstaged states are distinct: the reconstructed index represents the\n8021|staged version, while the working-tree manifest represents actual working\n8022|bytes. A clean-looking working tree with a wrong index is a failed\n8023|materialization.\n8024|For a submodule, the superproject HEAD-tree pointer, stage-0 index\n8025|<code>gitlink_oid</code>, and checked-out <code>head.oid</code> MUST reproduce the\n8026|exact clean/staged/unstaged combination in Section 10.4.\n8027|\n8028|### 12.4 Non-Git managed trees\n8029|\n8030|A non-Git member is a complete managed tree manifest containing included\n8031|directories, files, symlinks, hardlinks, modes, and content digests. Capture\n8032|MUST verify the tree did not change between enumeration and digest completion,\n8033|or retry from the start.\n8034|\n8035|The destination MUST be absent, empty, or a matching managed replica. A\n8036|divergent tree follows Section 11.7. There is no directory timestamp merge.\n8037|\n8038|### 12.5 Agent project configuration\n8039|\n8040|Provider/project instruction files required to resume MUST be included when\n8041|they are inside the managed workspace and not secret-bearing. Examples include\n8042|repository-scoped agent instructions, provider settings explicitly approved for\n8043|the project, and task-board files for a local board.\n8044|\n8045|Global user configuration, plugins installed outside the workspace, API keys,\n8046|MCP OAuth tokens, keychains, and login state are machine-local. The destination\n8047|<code>doctor</code> check MUST report missing prerequisites rather than copying\n8048|them.\n8049|\n8050|### 12.6 Workspace groups and worktrees\n8051|\n8052|A group using <code>shared_checkout</code> is one atomic conflict and takeover\n8053|unit. All active sessions sharing it MUST quiesce, or graceful takeover MUST\n8054|fail with <code>workspace_group_busy</code>.\n8055|\n8056|Before either strategy, the coordinator computes the exact Section 11.3\n8057|WorkspaceGroupExpectation from immutable Session Records, authoritative\n8058|session Tombstones, winning leases, lifecycle state, and local managed-replica\n8059|markers. It MUST NOT read a mutable <code>session_ids</code> list from a group\n8060|record. Every phase revalidates the same expectation.\n8061|\n8062|With <code>workspace_mode = whole_group</code>, the coordinator creates one\n8063|group operation and a distinct idempotent handoff operation for every\n8064|member of\n8065|<code>MaterializationCohort.ownership_transfer_session_ids</code>. It quiesces\n8066|and stops exactly those cohort owners,\n8067|stages one workspace-group snapshot, prepares every successor lease, and only\n8068|then advances any lease. Destination resume is withheld until every cohort\n8069|lease has converged to that destination; a partial post-fence failure leaves\n8070|the affected destination sessions stopped and parks the old shared checkout\n8071|rather than resuming a subset against it.\n8072|\n8073|The operator MAY instead select <code>separate_worktrees</code>. The materializer then\n8074|creates one managed Git worktree per active logical session from the shared\n8075|object store, applies that session's index/working state, and records independent\n8076|destination paths. It MUST not point two active owners at one mutable worktree.\n8077|Managed-tree members become separate managed copies. Only the selected\n8078|session's lease moves; every non-migrating owner retains its source checkout.\n8079|\n8080|<code>WG-TAKEOVER-WHOLE-POS</code> starts with two active sessions sharing one\n8081|marker, uses one group operation/two session operations, stops both before the\n8082|first lease advance, installs one group snapshot, advances both leases, then\n8083|resumes both at the destination. <code>WG-TAKEOVER-WORKTREE-POS</code> moves one\n8084|session to a new marker/worktree and leaves the other lease and checkout\n8085|unchanged. <code>WG-TAKEOVER-JOIN-N1</code> adds a Session Record after prepare;\n8086|<code>WG-TAKEOVER-LEAVE-N1</code> adds an authoritative session Tombstone after\n8087|prepare. Both MUST fail with <code>workspace_group_changed</code> before any\n8088|lease advance; after an advance they enter the explicit partial group recovery\n8089|above and never silently shrink the cohort.\n8090|\n8091|## 13. End-to-end lifecycle flows\n8092|\n8093|Every mutating flow has a UUIDv7 <code>operation_id</code>. A retry with the\n8094|same operation ID and inputs MUST be idempotent. A retry with different inputs\n8095|MUST fail with <code>idempotency_mismatch</code>.\n8096|\n8097|### 13.1 Direct-session launch\n8098|\n8099|Preconditions:\n8100|\n8101|- configuration and local host identity are valid;\n8102|- the session name is unused across the reachable mesh;\n8103|- the provider plugin is trusted and <code>probe</code> succeeds;\n8104|- requested profile mapping is available;\n8105|- destination workspace is validated or captured as a new managed workspace;\n8106|- the terminal backend is available; and\n8107|- no task-board fields are supplied.\n8108|\n8109|Transitions:\n8110|\n8111|1. Allocate session, caller-stable bootstrap-operation,\n8112|   first-checkpoint-operation, and initial lease IDs. If the workspace has a\n8113|   valid current managed-replica marker, reuse its unique Workspace Group\n8114|   Record after exact topology validation; otherwise allocate new workspace-\n8115|   group/workspace IDs and one new group record. Epoch is 1 and the local host\n8116|   is the holder.\n8117|2. Persist any new Workspace Group Record, then the Session Record referencing\n8118|   that group, the initial Lease Record, and <code>session.created</code> carrying\n8119|   both operation IDs before creating a process. Joining\n8120|   an existing group publishes no group update; derived membership changes by\n8121|   union of the new Session Record.\n8122|3. Create the durable terminal entry <code>ax pane SESSION_ID</code> with the\n8123|   bootstrap operation ID.\n8124|4. Call provider <code>launch</code> and validate its argv/env-name plan.\n8125|5. Start the provider through the terminal backend with the epoch-1 fencing\n8126|   token and persisted profile. Persist <code>provider.launched</code> with\n8127|   <code>execution_profile</code> equal to the Session Record and\n8128|   <code>profile_source_event_id = null</code>.\n8129|6. Call <code>identify-session</code>; persist the Provider Identity Record and\n8130|   <code>provider.identified</code> event.\n8131|7. When the first safe boundary is observed, create the workspace-group root\n8132|   manifest. If <code>portable_store</code> is enabled, call provider\n8133|   <code>native-store-plan phase=capture</code> and <code>capture</code> with the\n8134|   first-checkpoint operation ID into an\n8135|   isolated Object Sink; otherwise create a zero-entry provider manifest that\n8136|   references the exact Provider Identity Record. Validate both, then publish\n8137|   the direct-path checkpoint with a non-null provider manifest.\n8138|8. Publish immutable objects for replica sync and report <code>running</code>\n8139|   or <code>idle</code>.\n8140|\n8141|The first-checkpoint operation ID keys the capture/export and checkpoint-\n8142|publication transaction even when a backend-only provider emits a zero-entry\n8143|manifest. An identical retry reuses the same immutable objects and event;\n8144|changing either operation ID after <code>session.created</code> is\n8145|<code>idempotency_mismatch</code>.\n8146|\n8147|Until step 7 publishes the first Checkpoint, this is the closed\n8148|<em>bootstrap window</em>. The epoch-1 lease is authoritative with\n8149|<code>checkpoint_id = null</code>; the derived session state is\n8150|<code>creating</code> while a retry is eligible and <code>failed</code> after an\n8151|ambiguous/live-process error or explicit abort. No implementation may invent a\n8152|checkpoint ID or call that state <code>stopped</code>.\n8153|\n8154|Failure handling is phase-exact:\n8155|\n8156|| Fixture/boundary | Durable facts | Recovery |\n8157|| --- | --- | --- |\n8158|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-BEFORE-TERMINAL</code> | Session/lease exist; no terminal, process, identity, or checkpoint | <code>ax resume</code> may retry terminal creation under the same IDs after probe; force stop emits bootstrap abort |\n8159|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-TERMINAL</code> | Wrapper exists; no provider process or checkpoint | Resume reuses that wrapper and retries launch once; force stop removes only the inert wrapper after closure proof |\n8160|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-PROCESS</code> | Provider may be live; no identified identity/checkpoint | Resume MUST first reconcile exact PID/PTY and bootstrap operation; if liveness/identity is ambiguous, remain failed and require stop/reconciliation rather than start a second process |\n8161|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-IDENTITY</code> | Identity/event exist; no checkpoint | Resume validates the same identity and continues capture; it MUST NOT call provider new-session launch |\n8162|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-BEFORE-CHECKPOINT</code> | Captured objects may exist but no Checkpoint Record is authoritative | Validate/reuse immutable objects and publish the first checkpoint, or abort; objects are never relabeled as a checkpoint |\n8163|\n8164|A bootstrap retry is allowed only at epoch 1, with a null lease checkpoint,\n8165|no authoritative <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code>, and either proven no\n8166|provider process or one exact reconciled process/identity from this launch.\n8167|The retry reuses every already persisted operation/terminal/provider identity\n8168|and is idempotent; it never creates a second raw provider process. A graceful\n8169|stop may first reach a safe boundary and publish the initial checkpoint, then\n8170|execute the ordinary checkpointed stop. A force stop may close the process and\n8171|emit <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> followed by\n8172|<code>session.stopped</code> with null checkpoint,\n8173|<code>resumable=false</code>, and <code>closure_kind=bootstrap_abort</code>; the\n8174|resulting lifecycle state is <code>failed</code>, not <code>stopped</code>.\n8175|Closure with an unknown/live process is unsuccessful and emits neither event.\n8176|\n8177|<code>BOOT-ABORT-CROSS-CONTRACT</code> is the exact successful force-closure\n8178|projection for a failure after process creation and before identity:\n8179|\n8180|| Surface | Exact authoritative values |\n8181|| --- | --- |\n8182|| <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> payload | <code>{operation_id:BOOTSTRAP_ID,failure_phase:after_process,provider_identity_record_id:null,manager_session_ref:null,process_closed:true,store_closed:true,resume_allowed:false}</code> |\n8183|| Following <code>session.stopped</code> payload | <code>{graceful:false,checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,closure_kind:bootstrap_abort,process_closed:true,store_closed:true}</code> |\n8184|| RPC <code>session.stop</code> success | <code>{operation_id:STOP_ID,already_applied:false,closure:{process_closed:true,store_closed:true,exit_code:EXIT_OR_NULL,remaining_process_handles:[],final_store_generation:null},final_checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,bootstrap_aborted:true,resulting_state:failed}</code> |\n8185|| CLI <code>stop</code> body | Exact failed <code>SessionSummary</code> with both newest-checkpoint members null, plus <code>{graceful:false,checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,bootstrap_aborted:true,process_closed:true,store_closed:true}</code> |\n8186|\n8187|The event IDs and sequences are ordinary Section 5.2 identities. The RPC and\n8188|CLI operation ID is STOP_ID; the abort event retains BOOTSTRAP_ID so audit can\n8189|join the abandoned launch. <code>BOOT-ABORT-N1</code> changes any one null to a\n8190|digest, any closure boolean to false, resulting state to stopped, or resumable\n8191|to true; the attempted success MUST be rejected. After identity, exactly the\n8192|direct provider-identity or task-board manager-reference member becomes\n8193|non-null as Section 5.2 requires.\n8194|\n8195|The command MUST NOT delete the workspace, native provider bytes, or immutable\n8196|records during recovery. The initial lease remains authoritative until an\n8197|explicit later ownership operation.\n8198|\n8199|### 13.2 Task-board session launch\n8200|\n8201|Preconditions are the direct launch preconditions except that the official\n8202|task-board bridge and the requested task-board capability MUST be available.\n8203|The logical board identity and, when applicable, exact\n8204|<code>board-goal-v2</code> reference/revision MUST be known. The Section 14.1\n8205|board ID/URL, launch mode, goal pair, and binding rules are the only public\n8206|resolution contract; provider ID or current directory MUST NOT silently choose\n8207|them.\n8208|\n8209|Transitions:\n8210|\n8211|1. Allocate distinct caller-stable bootstrap and first-checkpoint operation\n8212|   IDs. Create and durably persist the\n8213|   Workspace Group Record or reuse a marker-validated existing one exactly as\n8214|   Section 13.1, then create and durably persist the\n8215|   <code>kind = task_board</code> Session Record with creation-time\n8216|   <code>manager_session_ref = null</code>, the epoch-1 lease, and\n8217|   <code>session.created</code> carrying both IDs before asking task-board to\n8218|   create a process.\n8219|2. Invoke the Section 9.2 public bridge <code>launch</code> command with the\n8220|   bootstrap operation ID, the exact persisted lease/profile/provider/mode/goal/launch\n8221|   plan, logical workspace mapping, and no provider-private mutation.\n8222|3. Task-board returns a public manager session reference, exact provider\n8223|   version, ax binding, and capability result. Validate every repeated input,\n8224|   then persist\n8225|   <code>task_board.launched</code> with the Session Record profile and\n8226|   <code>profile_source_event_id = null</code>; this event, not an edit to the Session\n8227|   Record, establishes the current manager reference.\n8228|4. At a manager-proven safe boundary, invoke official\n8229|   <code>session export --mode snapshot</code> with the persisted first-checkpoint\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
